 "reading_text": "The user is asking whether the image shows two small pulmonary arterioles packed with laminated swirls of fetal squamous cells. Let me look carefully at the two H&E panels.\n\nTop panel: a small vessel (arteriole) with a thickened, layered wall. The lumen contains a dense pink/eosinophilic mass with some clear vacuoles (clefts) at the edges. The wall shows concentric lamellation. There's also some cellular debris.\n\nBottom panel: two small vessels, each lumen filled with concentric laminated eosinophilic swirls — whorled, layered material. Red blood cells in surrounding capillaries.\n\nThe question phrasing \"laminated swirls of fetal squamous cells\" is a specific histopathological description. This pattern — arterioles (or small vessels) occluded by laminated, whorled eosinophilic material that represents desquamated squamous cells — is characteristic of fetal squamous cell embolization / amniotic fluid embolism? Actually, amniotic fluid embolism classically shows squamous cells, vernix caseosa, lanugo hair, mucin, etc. in fetal capillaries or pulmonary vessels. But \"laminated swirls of fetal squamous cells\" specifically describes squamous cell emboli in small pulmonary vessels.\n\n",
